			<div class="content" id="post-content-4854132">
									

<p>Hey there! Thank you so much for taking the time to go through the concept. I'm glad you like the core. Allow me to address some of the points you made.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;My&nbsp;feedback</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Collapse</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I really like this idea of reading the bones but I am unconvinced about the entire thing centred around a vague K-class event. That's no way to build stakes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm trying to go for the idea that the SCP believes it, with the Foundation very much pointing out their scepticism at the validity of the information. <strong>This is not at all what the skip would be centred around.</strong> It'd serve to introduce the idea that the use of its abilities has shifted its worldview considerably (I mean, even in real life, we have people believing in various apocalypses), with further interviews reinforcing it. However, if you still think that wouldn't work, I get it. Some ideas from the top of my head could be the SCP worried about something/someone &quot;corrupting the Song of the world&quot; and wanting help from the Foundation, but that might be too vague. My (way back) original idea was the SCP being fascinated by the paranormal and wanting to assist and work in the Foundation, but I feared that would be too X-many - something I was terrified of getting close to.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>also not convinced that being sure the world would end would then lead to these results… like maybe the cooperation, but why would it make the SCP apathetic about the individual lives? That's fine if you wanna make them cold towards individual lives, but it doesn't make much sense this would be the reason personally.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I was thinking it would be along the lines of &quot;if everyone is going to be dead soon, no reason to get attached or care&quot;, or how in an apocalyptic scenario, which the SCP believes in, you'd not get too close to people as they could die at any moment. Still, it could easily be changed to a personality trait gained from being in contact with so many memories, including those of deaths.</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>I don't think experiment logs are the way to go here. Instead, you could summarize what research was done with the results and then unpack them in conversations or observation reports from the researchers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I probably worded that part badly since that's what I was trying to go for. The first set of logs would be with the SCP - Foundation giving it various bones and seeing how much info it can gain from them, experimenting with the species of the bones, how old they are, how mundane they are, as well as how accurately the SCP can glean information by cross-referencing the info. The second set of logs would be D-class being given the &quot;charms&quot; and observing how they handle it - a normal person being exposed to memories of, say, a horse, or a murder victim. Also what psychological traits influence how the &quot;charms&quot; affect people (ie. how does someone with would aphantasia deal with having different memories)</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>I think some unpacking of the actual anomaly would be more interesting than the focus on some vague end of the world.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, I think I just phrased it really badly since the other gentleperson above also got that impression. The K-class is <strong>by no means</strong> the focus of the skip - it's there to explain why the SCP turned in to the Foundation and what motivates it, but beyond the Foundation expressing strong scepticism towards these claims and introducing the idea of the SCP being affected by its abilities, that's it. The meat of the story would very much be talking to the SCP and seeing how its abilities work on it and others.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Please, let me know if any of these replies convinced you/better explained about some part of the skip, or if you're still not sold about some of them working, so I can start thinking of ways to change and modify the concepts. Again, thank you very much for taking the time!</p>

							</div>

								
					
