<blockquote>
<p>If it is really a bad idea, I could work out some sort of &quot;punishment&quot; or &quot;bad thing&quot; that would happen if within a certain time span no person chooses out of their own free will to take part in the trial.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This doesn't make it better: the issue is that the book is compelling itself to be used rather than there like&#8230;being a reason for the foundation to choose to use it. You're just, abstracting the force of the choice from 'the book gives literally no choice' to 'the book gives effectively no choice' since like, you have to use it to avoid the punishment/bad thing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I really intend to make the tests as impactful and individual as possible. So there is an individual trial for each person with no reoccurring tests from past participants. I plan to make it a big emotional arc with character growth.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What are the tests? How are they impactful? What's the big emotional arc with character growth? How does one test flow to the next in the narrative here?</p>

