			<div class="content" id="post-content-4854116">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>My concern is that there feels like a big disconnect between 'thing what attacks you if you don't run up the stairs in the dark' and everything about there being some kind of breakthrough that could unlock greater human potential, and between that and the tie in to SCP-5000 and Project PNEUMA. It ends up feeling like this has a lot of interesting little ideas in it but not enough connective tissue to hold it all together or to like, explain the jump from one angle to the next.</p>
<p>I'm also not sure if there's enough narrative meat to like, keep us enticed during this process? Like, even if you do manage to have all the information flow together well, it feels&#8230;like this really lacking a big narrative hook to really keep us unboard and prevent this feeling like a big info dump about a weird thing. Part of me wonders whether it might work best as a tale focussed around e.g. a foundation researcher who discovers the start of this and then gradually pieces a lot of disparate information together until they discover the truth of this?</p>
<p>I kinda feel like ending on the mystery of what the Foundation learnt might feel really unsatisfying: it kinda feels like the whole thing is built around this mystery and making the reader think about it, and not having a solid answer here feels&#8230;like it might end up feeling kinda analogous to reading every chapter of an Agatha Christie book except the final one where you find out who done it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

